  "Number": 12,
  "Question": "The lines \\(x=\\tfrac14 y+a\\) and \\(y=\\tfrac14 x+b\\) intersect at the point \\((1,2).\\) What is \\(a+b?\\)",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(0\\)",
    "B": "\\(\\dfrac{3}{4}\\)",
    "C": "\\(1\\)",
    "D": "\\(2\\)",
    "E": "\\(\\dfrac{9}{4}\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "E",
  "Solution": "The intersection point lies on both lines, so \\(x=1\\) and \\(y=2\\) satisfy both equations. Substituting gives\n\\[\n1=\\frac14(2)+a,\\qquad 2=\\frac14(1)+b.\n\\]\nTherefore\n\\[\na=1-\\frac12=\\frac12,\\qquad b=2-\\frac14=\\frac74.\n\\]\nThus \\(a+b=\\frac12+\\frac74=\\boxed{\\frac94}\\), choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{E}}\\).",
